WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.935
- I like knowing that I
can be there for people

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:04.143
without actually being there.

00:00:04.143 --> 00:00:04.976
That's a nice feeling.

00:00:04.976 --> 00:00:07.644
I've always been a very
honest, open, writer.

00:00:07.644 --> 00:00:09.248
I've always wore my heart on my sleeve.

00:00:09.248 --> 00:00:10.848
My voice is my instrument,

00:00:10.848 --> 00:00:13.695
so sometimes I sing
things that sound crazy,

00:00:13.695 --> 00:00:16.362
but then like one take out of 20

00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:19.204
will be really, really cool.

00:00:19.204 --> 00:00:20.762
- Hello Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:20.762 --> 00:00:22.372
My one word is believe,

00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:23.686
and I believe in people

00:00:23.686 --> 00:00:25.442
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:25.442 --> 00:00:26.785
And my sincere hope is that

00:00:26.785 --> 00:00:29.935
if you see in yourself what I see in you,

00:00:29.935 --> 00:00:31.840
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.996
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:32.996 --> 00:00:35.167
today we're going to learn
from Grammy award-winning

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.406
singer and songwriter Ellie Goulding,

00:00:37.406 --> 00:00:40.296
and my take on her top
ten rules to success.

00:00:40.296 --> 00:00:41.982
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:41.982 --> 00:00:44.343
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:44.343 --> 00:00:45.465
Also, as you're watching,

00:00:45.465 --> 00:00:47.529
if you hear something that
really resonates with you

00:00:47.529 --> 00:00:49.166
please leave it down
in the comments below,

00:00:49.166 --> 00:00:49.999
and put quotes around it

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:52.110
so other people can be
inspired by it as well.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.950
And if you leave it within
the first couple of hours

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.153
of this video going live,

00:00:55.153 --> 00:00:58.091
you have a chance to win
one of two daily prizes.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:01.077
(jet engine)

00:01:01.077 --> 00:01:03.660
(upbeat music)

00:01:11.101 --> 00:01:14.957
- Often fans tell me that the music I make

00:01:14.957 --> 00:01:16.715
has helped them through things

00:01:16.715 --> 00:01:18.599
with their family and their friends,

00:01:18.599 --> 00:01:19.976
and boyfriend/girlfriend.

00:01:19.976 --> 00:01:24.510
Help them reconcile with their
father or mother, or whoever.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.031
That's quite a nice feeling,

00:01:26.031 --> 00:01:27.646
that my music can do that.

00:01:27.646 --> 00:01:30.601
♫ 'Cause this is devotion, I am ...

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:32.905
I like knowing that I
can be there for people

00:01:32.905 --> 00:01:34.093
without actually being there.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:36.361
That's a nice feeling.

00:01:36.361 --> 00:01:37.507
I can't even be there for my own family,

00:01:37.507 --> 00:01:38.340
let alone my fans.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.899
So it's nice I can produce
something that is comforting.

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:44.566
♫ Break, I'll fix it for you.

00:01:44.566 --> 00:01:47.892
♫ I'm the one who drives to you at night

00:01:47.892 --> 00:01:51.542
♫ And maybe we'll fall a little deeper

00:01:51.542 --> 00:01:54.624
♫ I think our little
hearts will be alright

00:01:54.624 --> 00:01:58.124
♫ 'Cause this is devotion

00:02:01.261 --> 00:02:04.178
♫ This is devotion

00:02:07.124 --> 00:02:10.041
♫ This is devotion

00:02:11.134 --> 00:02:14.967
My writing style has
always been very hopeful.

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:19.851
I don't like writing depressing songs,

00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:22.549
unless I can offer some
kind of resolution.

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:24.396
And that's just how I've always written.

00:02:24.396 --> 00:02:26.812
Even when I used to
write poetry and essays,

00:02:26.812 --> 00:02:28.336
and shit when I was young.

00:02:28.336 --> 00:02:30.464
I write my songs in a very specific way.

00:02:30.464 --> 00:02:32.767
Like even if they're about someone.

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:36.544
It will never be like, ooh that's about;

00:02:36.544 --> 00:02:38.900
you know like it would never be;

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.691
It's never like shade or burn,

00:02:41.691 --> 00:02:43.500
or any of that kind of stuff.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.573
I don't believe in that.

00:02:44.573 --> 00:02:46.906
I believe in; I write songs,

00:02:48.075 --> 00:02:49.514
I like to write songs with a resolution.

00:02:49.514 --> 00:02:52.931
I like to write songs always
with something positive.

00:02:52.931 --> 00:02:55.451
So even if I write about
somebody that's broken my heart,

00:02:55.451 --> 00:02:57.312
or somebody that's hurt me,

00:02:57.312 --> 00:02:59.588
or a situation I've been
in that's been unpleasant.

00:02:59.588 --> 00:03:02.909
I still want to try and write like a poet.

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:06.241
I still want to write
something that is timeless.

00:03:06.241 --> 00:03:07.336
Like Shakespeare.

00:03:07.336 --> 00:03:11.167
I want to write things
that sound true forever.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:12.725
I see the good in everyone,

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:13.837
and I see the good in everything,

00:03:13.837 --> 00:03:16.310
and I think that there's
always a silver lining

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:20.183
and there's always good
to come out of everything.

00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:22.635
So if you notice my lyrics,

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.589
there's always a resolution of some kind.

00:03:24.589 --> 00:03:29.211
There's always an everything
is going to be okay moment.

00:03:29.211 --> 00:03:31.527
So even if I have been in situations

00:03:31.527 --> 00:03:33.072
where I think that was really unfair,

00:03:33.072 --> 00:03:34.239
that was mean,

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:37.907
or I was hurt by something.

00:03:37.907 --> 00:03:39.808
I always try and do it
in a way that resolves it

00:03:39.808 --> 00:03:40.989
in my own head.

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.786
I think that; I think that
as a writer I've progressed.

00:03:44.786 --> 00:03:47.429
I've always been a very
honest, open writer.

00:03:47.429 --> 00:03:49.477
I've always worn my heart on my sleeve,

00:03:49.477 --> 00:03:51.919
the way that I write
lyrics, and tell stories.

00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:53.728
Without wanting to sound like a cliche,

00:03:53.728 --> 00:03:55.855
I genuinely like being very honest

00:03:55.855 --> 00:03:57.645
and being real about my situation,

00:03:57.645 --> 00:04:00.631
about my childhood, about my past.

00:04:00.631 --> 00:04:02.557
It kind of contributes to what I write,

00:04:02.557 --> 00:04:04.577
and what I do as an artist.

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:06.162
So I think it's important to shed light

00:04:06.162 --> 00:04:09.597
on what I've been through and stuff.

00:04:09.597 --> 00:04:12.486
And I never; I always feel
weird if I leave stuff out,

00:04:12.486 --> 00:04:16.236
and fans are left not
knowing stuff about me.

00:04:17.408 --> 00:04:18.735
I'd like them to to
kind of know everything.

00:04:18.735 --> 00:04:20.815
I did listen to a bit of Joni Mitchell.

00:04:20.815 --> 00:04:24.348
Did listen to; I have
listened to a lot of Feist.

00:04:24.348 --> 00:04:28.401
My Brightest Diamond,
and I love the (mumbles)

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.886
I really like their lyrics.

00:04:29.886 --> 00:04:31.645
And suddenly I realized
that you could be quirky,

00:04:31.645 --> 00:04:33.692
but you could also still be quite poppy.

00:04:33.692 --> 00:04:36.879
My mom constantly listened
to pop and dance music.

00:04:36.879 --> 00:04:37.787
So it was very pop,

00:04:37.787 --> 00:04:41.204
and I wasn't introduced to any old bands.

00:04:42.977 --> 00:04:44.494
I was discovering music by myself.

00:04:44.494 --> 00:04:46.299
So it was kind of a discovery;

00:04:46.299 --> 00:04:50.387
it was me discovering things
that would allow me to

00:04:50.387 --> 00:04:53.338
find out about older
bands and older singers.

00:04:53.338 --> 00:04:57.484
Maybe it could be that I
have a very kind of unique

00:04:57.484 --> 00:05:00.104
sort of way, in that I've
listened to a hell of a lot

00:05:00.104 --> 00:05:03.354
of quite obscure folk music, and Bjork.

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:07.827
But then I also really love pop music.

00:05:07.827 --> 00:05:10.445
But I refuse to let one of
them overtake the other.

00:05:10.445 --> 00:05:14.129
So I think that's why I
seem to have been given this

00:05:14.129 --> 00:05:16.046
appreciation in blocks.

00:05:19.186 --> 00:05:21.853
Sort of a more of an Indie area.

00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:27.511
And then also I seem to have
this sort of pop thing as well.

00:05:27.511 --> 00:05:30.795
I guess my lyrics are just
really important to me.

00:05:30.795 --> 00:05:33.549
I never want them to be
too alien and complicated,

00:05:33.549 --> 00:05:37.067
but I want them to have a meaning always.

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:40.404
It's like it's always finding
the right kind of balance

00:05:40.404 --> 00:05:42.737
in my songs of surreal stuff

00:05:44.757 --> 00:05:46.123
and then very real stuff

00:05:46.123 --> 00:05:49.392
that's very universal and relatable.

00:05:49.392 --> 00:05:52.781
So for me it's always finding
that balance within my tracks.

00:05:52.781 --> 00:05:54.689
I write things on my laptop a lot.

00:05:54.689 --> 00:05:56.333
And now that I've got a laptop.

00:05:56.333 --> 00:05:58.116
And I write on my phone.

00:05:58.116 --> 00:05:59.767
I write on paper.

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:01.913
It depends sometimes;

00:06:01.913 --> 00:06:05.625
I kind of have to be in a
certain frame of mind to write.

00:06:05.625 --> 00:06:08.972
I have to be; sometimes
I have to be quite down,

00:06:08.972 --> 00:06:10.697
sometimes I have to be really elated.

00:06:10.697 --> 00:06:14.317
But generally, it's me
coming up with some chords

00:06:14.317 --> 00:06:18.229
and some words, and something
I really want to write about,

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:19.728
something I need to get off my chest.

00:06:19.728 --> 00:06:23.921
- What do you think that
this panel is seeing in you,

00:06:23.921 --> 00:06:26.107
they have decided that you hit the moment.

00:06:26.107 --> 00:06:27.357
- I don't know.

00:06:28.866 --> 00:06:31.337
And I wish I did, because
it would be a lot easier.

00:06:31.337 --> 00:06:32.720
I suppose I'm quite normal,

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.638
and I wasn't necessarily ambitious

00:06:34.638 --> 00:06:37.451
so I guess I don't really
have a business kind of mind.

00:06:37.451 --> 00:06:39.446
So in that way my music ends up being;

00:06:39.446 --> 00:06:40.924
I hate using this word, but quite organic

00:06:40.924 --> 00:06:43.402
in that I write things;

00:06:43.402 --> 00:06:45.943
I just go after it blind
and just hope for the best.

00:06:45.943 --> 00:06:48.183
And that's how I've always worked.

00:06:48.183 --> 00:06:52.211
I sort of like to do things in the moment.

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:54.847
So if I have a resolution I want to make,

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.597
I just kind of do it.

00:06:57.447 --> 00:06:59.168
Actually, you know,

00:06:59.168 --> 00:07:01.823
I want to be a bit more
adventurous next year.

00:07:01.823 --> 00:07:06.781
As in I want to be a bit
more of an adventure seeker,

00:07:06.781 --> 00:07:08.547
do some skydives and stuff.

00:07:08.547 --> 00:07:10.083
But I don't think I can do a bungee jump.

00:07:10.083 --> 00:07:13.124
I think that's a bit too far.

00:07:13.124 --> 00:07:16.570
- You can skydive, but
you couldn't bungee jump.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:17.906
- I think it's very different.

00:07:17.906 --> 00:07:19.840
Bungee jumping you throw yourself off.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.175
At least skydive, someone's
going to (mumbles)

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:25.461
and that's that, and
just takes you with them.

00:07:25.461 --> 00:07:27.293
I think bungee jumping is
a while different thing.

00:07:27.293 --> 00:07:30.494
But I think I want to do
things that kind of scare me,

00:07:30.494 --> 00:07:32.547
and get over my fears
a bit more next year.

00:07:32.547 --> 00:07:35.073
So maybe I do have some New
Year's resolutions actually.

00:07:35.073 --> 00:07:36.832
I've been supporting Bruno Mars,

00:07:36.832 --> 00:07:39.796
and I supported quite a
few artists in arenas,

00:07:39.796 --> 00:07:42.358
so I feel like I've been
lucky in that respect

00:07:42.358 --> 00:07:45.660
because I've built a
stamina that's required

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.052
of playing such big shows,

00:07:47.052 --> 00:07:48.042
but they haven't been my shows,

00:07:48.042 --> 00:07:49.006
so the pressure's been off.

00:07:49.006 --> 00:07:52.969
So I've been able to practice
playing to lots of people,

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:56.752
but like I said the
pressure is off in that

00:07:56.752 --> 00:07:59.069
it's not my own show, and
I can sort of, I dunno.

00:07:59.069 --> 00:08:03.507
It's definitely easier
supporting someone in an arena.

00:08:03.507 --> 00:08:07.080
Like the crowd is not
necessarily there to support you,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:08.113
but you can win them over.

00:08:08.113 --> 00:08:10.069
It makes you realize that you have to

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:11.791
put on a really special show,

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.869
to get people's favor.

00:08:13.869 --> 00:08:15.157
So I've been able to do that.

00:08:15.157 --> 00:08:17.778
And then my own shows; I go
back and do my own shows,

00:08:17.778 --> 00:08:20.916
and I sort of replicate
what I do in arenas,

00:08:20.916 --> 00:08:22.578
and it makes the show a bit bigger,

00:08:22.578 --> 00:08:23.601
and a bit more special.

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:26.184
- What is it like as a
writer, as a musician,

00:08:26.184 --> 00:08:28.031
at a time when there is this.

00:08:28.031 --> 00:08:29.750
I mean once upon a
time, I'm showing my age

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.705
I apologize, but I can't help it.

00:08:31.705 --> 00:08:32.989
Something like you would have come along,

00:08:32.989 --> 00:08:34.722
and in a way there would
have been one of it,

00:08:34.722 --> 00:08:35.837
maybe two.

00:08:35.837 --> 00:08:38.581
Whereas now, and not to say
they're exactly the same,

00:08:38.581 --> 00:08:39.479
but you know what I'm saying,

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:42.568
there can be a lot of the
same kind of thing, of peers.

00:08:42.568 --> 00:08:43.925
So I wondered how difficult it is for you

00:08:43.925 --> 00:08:46.265
to try and generate
the kind of originality

00:08:46.265 --> 00:08:47.657
that you really want to have.

00:08:47.657 --> 00:08:51.338
- Well I think the important
thing is not to try.

00:08:51.338 --> 00:08:52.990
And hopefully you don't have to try.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:54.632
And luckily I feel like I've been given

00:08:54.632 --> 00:08:56.855
that kind of advantage,
in that I feel like I'm

00:08:56.855 --> 00:08:59.113
not trying hard to create
something that's original.

00:08:59.113 --> 00:09:02.710
But because I have such
a passion for folk music,

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:06.564
and music which is so simple and beautiful

00:09:06.564 --> 00:09:07.760
with just a guitar,

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.724
that it just so happens that
I've constructed my songs

00:09:10.724 --> 00:09:12.660
that way without thinking
about it too much.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.827
Not putting too much, this
song has to be different

00:09:18.063 --> 00:09:20.128
but it also has to be mainstream
so that I sell records.

00:09:20.128 --> 00:09:21.552
It's completely not like that.

00:09:21.552 --> 00:09:25.206
It's just the fact that myself
and my producer Starsmith,

00:09:25.206 --> 00:09:26.099
he's the same age,

00:09:26.099 --> 00:09:28.466
and he has a passion for electronic music.

00:09:28.466 --> 00:09:30.776
I have a passion for
folk music in particular.

00:09:30.776 --> 00:09:32.365
But we also listen to a
lot of the same things

00:09:32.365 --> 00:09:33.375
when we were younger.

00:09:33.375 --> 00:09:36.526
So we just go into a
session and slowly start to

00:09:36.526 --> 00:09:38.962
construct a song, and I
usually already have the

00:09:38.962 --> 00:09:41.695
kind of the backbone for it.

00:09:41.695 --> 00:09:44.413
But I think it's easy
enough for me to say this,

00:09:44.413 --> 00:09:47.186
but I think the best
thing is not to try hard,

00:09:47.186 --> 00:09:48.606
because you can almost tell.

00:09:48.606 --> 00:09:50.806
I think that's why the
break of connection between

00:09:50.806 --> 00:09:53.272
you and your listeners,

00:09:53.272 --> 00:09:55.393
that's when the break comes
because they can't connect.

00:09:55.393 --> 00:09:57.128
'Cause they can almost sense

00:09:57.128 --> 00:09:59.424
that it's like too try hard or whatever.

00:09:59.424 --> 00:10:02.308
It's key to everything,
or it's key to what I do.

00:10:02.308 --> 00:10:06.699
I wouldn't be here without
experimenting like crazy.

00:10:06.699 --> 00:10:09.541
I like so many different types of music,

00:10:09.541 --> 00:10:12.469
and I hope that I could
get away with including

00:10:12.469 --> 00:10:15.012
all of those different
influences on my first record.

00:10:15.012 --> 00:10:17.075
And I think I got away with it.

00:10:17.075 --> 00:10:18.625
And maybe on my second as well.

00:10:18.625 --> 00:10:22.941
But I mean like, I would
say that I'm a pop artist,

00:10:22.941 --> 00:10:24.358
I make pop music.

00:10:26.595 --> 00:10:28.809
But it's not without a
lot of experimentation

00:10:28.809 --> 00:10:31.642
and also risking writing something

00:10:33.035 --> 00:10:35.285
that maybe isn't very good,

00:10:37.043 --> 00:10:38.623
or is silly.

00:10:38.623 --> 00:10:40.247
My voice is my instrument

00:10:40.247 --> 00:10:42.929
so sometimes I sing things
that just sound crazy,

00:10:42.929 --> 00:10:45.846
but then one take out of 20 will be

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:48.946
really, really cool, and
then I keep that one.

00:10:48.946 --> 00:10:49.779
Then that's that.

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:52.148
So yeah, experimentation
is everything for me

00:10:52.148 --> 00:10:53.294
as a musician, yeah.

00:10:53.294 --> 00:10:54.682
- When I tweeted today

00:10:54.682 --> 00:10:57.029
that you were going to
do another interview,

00:10:57.029 --> 00:10:59.529
we got all kinds of questions.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:01.209
One of them was, how do you feel about

00:11:01.209 --> 00:11:03.396
so many people remixing Starry Eyes?

00:11:03.396 --> 00:11:04.551
- Well a lot of people have remixed that,

00:11:04.551 --> 00:11:06.029
and a lot of people have remixed;

00:11:06.029 --> 00:11:08.125
Ones that just kind of
blown up out of nowhere,

00:11:08.125 --> 00:11:09.310
and it's amazing.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:10.545
I love hearing my song

00:11:10.545 --> 00:11:12.074
with a completely different feel to it.

00:11:12.074 --> 00:11:13.939
I love the idea that my
vocal can just be put

00:11:13.939 --> 00:11:16.044
onto pretty much anything.

00:11:16.044 --> 00:11:19.443
- Lights has been remixed by
every single artist right now.

00:11:19.443 --> 00:11:20.276
On the planet, it seems like.

00:11:20.276 --> 00:11:23.296
Do you have a favorite version?

00:11:23.296 --> 00:11:25.292
- Bassnectar remix has
always been my favorite.

00:11:25.292 --> 00:11:27.959
He's like my big brother, Lorin.

00:11:29.138 --> 00:11:30.939
It's so funny how the remix
started off our friendship,

00:11:30.939 --> 00:11:35.526
but it really kick started
Lights in the States.

00:11:35.526 --> 00:11:39.026
'Cause at first when Lights started having

00:11:40.009 --> 00:11:41.833
some kind of prominence in America,

00:11:41.833 --> 00:11:45.059
it was always like, "Oh yo,
I love the Bassnectar remix."

00:11:45.059 --> 00:11:47.435
At first, and then all of a
sudden Lights just creeping

00:11:47.435 --> 00:11:49.935
up and it had its own journey.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.423
So yeah, there's probably about

00:11:55.394 --> 00:11:57.863
1,000 Lights remixes out now.

00:11:57.863 --> 00:11:59.144
I'm not even joking.

00:11:59.144 --> 00:12:01.857
It's the most remixed
song probably of all time.

00:12:01.857 --> 00:12:04.886
I've been probably the most
remixed artist in the UK.

00:12:04.886 --> 00:12:06.860
For some reason I'm
just like, that's okay.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:07.999
But then some I listen to,

00:12:07.999 --> 00:12:10.330
I think that is better than the original.

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:12.453
And I really fall in love with them.

00:12:12.453 --> 00:12:14.339
Like a guy from Monsieur Adi from France,

00:12:14.339 --> 00:12:18.506
remixed Guns and Horses,
and it just blew me away.

00:12:19.738 --> 00:12:22.143
I thought, God it sounded
like it was supposed to be

00:12:22.143 --> 00:12:22.976
at the end of a film.

00:12:22.976 --> 00:12:26.046
Like some epic, a love story,
because it was amazing.

00:12:26.046 --> 00:12:28.613
It made me cry a little bit.

00:12:28.613 --> 00:12:30.007
It's almost like it
wasn't my song anymore.

00:12:30.007 --> 00:12:32.368
It was just an amazing thing
that someone had created.

00:12:32.368 --> 00:12:35.051
I've read a lot since I was young,

00:12:35.051 --> 00:12:39.773
so books inspire me,
like (mumbles), poetry.

00:12:39.773 --> 00:12:43.559
I've read a lot of poetry
over the last few years.

00:12:43.559 --> 00:12:47.726
And I like kind of dark, like
funny, kind of humorous stuff.

00:12:50.858 --> 00:12:54.525
I read a big book of
poems at Staying Alive,

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.227
a compilation of poems.

00:12:59.227 --> 00:13:03.389
They're like real and
raw, and quite explicit.

00:13:03.389 --> 00:13:05.056
And so I used those.

00:13:06.799 --> 00:13:09.783
Obviously like other singers
of the band, musicians,

00:13:09.783 --> 00:13:11.700
I like classical music.

00:13:12.663 --> 00:13:14.298
I listen to so much stuff

00:13:14.298 --> 00:13:16.361
that I'm just kind of
inundated all the time.

00:13:16.361 --> 00:13:18.910
So that's kind of how I work really.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:20.972
Listen to a lot of music all the time.

00:13:20.972 --> 00:13:23.808
Now it's just, internet
is used by so many people,

00:13:23.808 --> 00:13:25.883
and there's so much downloading

00:13:25.883 --> 00:13:27.942
that it's become the way.

00:13:27.942 --> 00:13:29.306
And more people are getting iPods,

00:13:29.306 --> 00:13:30.841
more people are finding their music.

00:13:30.841 --> 00:13:32.446
And so it's kind of they're more attracted

00:13:32.446 --> 00:13:33.946
to internet stuff.

00:13:35.031 --> 00:13:37.761
I guess there's more
ways of finding music.

00:13:37.761 --> 00:13:38.978
You can get things like Track in a Box.

00:13:38.978 --> 00:13:41.282
You get a track sent to you every day.

00:13:41.282 --> 00:13:42.806
And you find out things that way.

00:13:42.806 --> 00:13:44.851
And me personally, I do feel like

00:13:44.851 --> 00:13:47.465
there's so much music,

00:13:47.465 --> 00:13:50.841
it's impossible for me
as a maker of new music,

00:13:50.841 --> 00:13:53.762
to be completely aware of everything.

00:13:53.762 --> 00:13:56.359
I just go on random recommendations
that people give me,

00:13:56.359 --> 00:13:58.270
and I might discover something for myself.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.480
But I think that's the best way,

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:01.528
because you can get just inundated with;

00:14:01.528 --> 00:14:04.360
you just get so much stuff thrown at you.

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:05.400
I can beat box.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.196
- Oh yeah, well forget that.

00:14:07.196 --> 00:14:09.254
- A little bit, probably
not as good, I dunno.

00:14:09.254 --> 00:14:10.087
- Go ahead, try.

00:14:10.087 --> 00:14:12.587
(beat boxing)

00:14:13.564 --> 00:14:16.322
- That was a little one.

00:14:16.322 --> 00:14:17.155
- Oh no.

00:14:17.155 --> 00:14:17.988
- It's getting that.

00:14:17.988 --> 00:14:20.488
(beat boxing)

00:14:24.878 --> 00:14:26.277
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:26.277 --> 00:14:29.538
I made this video because
Shailendra Einstein asked me to.

00:14:29.538 --> 00:14:30.820
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.146
and their future top ten,

00:14:32.146 --> 00:14:34.523
please check out the link
down in the description below,

00:14:34.523 --> 00:14:37.740
and you can cast your vote
on who we should do next.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:40.359
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Marta Magdalena.

00:14:40.359 --> 00:14:42.769
Marta thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:14:42.769 --> 00:14:45.713
Your One Word, and posting that
awesome message on Twitter.

00:14:45.713 --> 00:14:48.052
I'm so glad that you're coming
back to it and rereading it

00:14:48.052 --> 00:14:49.960
whenever you're having moments of doubt.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:52.317
I really appreciate that,
and for sharing it as well.

00:14:52.317 --> 00:14:53.928
Thank you, thank you, thank you so much,

00:14:53.928 --> 00:14:55.484
and I'm really happy you enjoyed the book.

00:14:55.484 --> 00:14:56.848
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:56.848 --> 00:14:57.964
I believe in you.

00:14:57.964 --> 00:14:59.965
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:14:59.965 --> 00:15:01.826
and whatever your one word is.

00:15:01.826 --> 00:15:02.996
Much love.

00:15:02.996 --> 00:15:04.644
I'll see you soon.

00:15:04.644 --> 00:15:07.061
(jet engine)

00:15:10.247 --> 00:15:11.636
- How have your (mumbles) going?

00:15:11.636 --> 00:15:13.856
Your sound's evolved a
lot since the first album.

00:15:13.856 --> 00:15:15.958
How's it been taken?

00:15:15.958 --> 00:15:17.557
- It's pretty good.

00:15:17.557 --> 00:15:20.379
The second album has
gotten me around the world,

00:15:20.379 --> 00:15:22.712
and gotten me some, I dunno,

00:15:24.463 --> 00:15:26.934
to go some cool places.

00:15:26.934 --> 00:15:29.085
It's definitely, I think
everything's evolved.

00:15:29.085 --> 00:15:31.502
Like I've managed to sort of,

00:15:33.072 --> 00:15:34.891
get worldwide a bit more,

00:15:34.891 --> 00:15:37.425
and tour places I've never toured before.

00:15:37.425 --> 00:15:38.948
And it's all evolved in general.

00:15:38.948 --> 00:15:43.086
Like touring, and the
records, and just everything.

00:15:43.086 --> 00:15:43.919
I don't know really.

00:15:43.919 --> 00:15:46.701
I feel like I do work really hard,

00:15:46.701 --> 00:15:48.706
but I sort of stay at a level

00:15:48.706 --> 00:15:50.683
that I'm really
comfortable and happy with.

00:15:50.683 --> 00:15:53.700
I genuinely never wanted
to be a superstar.

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:54.962
I just didn't.

00:15:54.962 --> 00:15:56.474
It just never really occurred to me

00:15:56.474 --> 00:15:58.035
that that would ever be possible.

00:15:58.035 --> 00:15:59.981
I'm grateful for everyday,

00:15:59.981 --> 00:16:04.689
because I get to support
Bruno, fricking, Mars on tour.

00:16:04.689 --> 00:16:06.094
I just don't understand it.

00:16:06.094 --> 00:16:08.886
So long as I have that,
which I do everyday,

00:16:08.886 --> 00:16:10.413
I just have the best time.

00:16:10.413 --> 00:16:13.462
I get to write songs and
put them on a record.

00:16:13.462 --> 00:16:15.824
Some people buy them,
and some people don't.

00:16:15.824 --> 00:16:19.046
I have a really fun time with it.

00:16:19.046 --> 00:16:21.355
The biggest thing I probably go to,

00:16:21.355 --> 00:16:24.855
if I'm kind of in a rush, is Hype Machine.

00:16:26.357 --> 00:16:27.675
I go on Hype Machine a lot.

00:16:27.675 --> 00:16:31.110
I go on EARMILK, (mumbles) quite a lot.

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:32.763
But you know, I feel like I'm very lucky,

00:16:32.763 --> 00:16:36.668
because I've been connected
with so many producers and DJs

00:16:36.668 --> 00:16:39.741
just from remixes and
from working with people,

00:16:39.741 --> 00:16:43.450
and having such an affinity
with electronic music.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:46.733
I get to meet people,
and people send me stuff.

00:16:46.733 --> 00:16:47.895
I have connections with a lot of people.

00:16:47.895 --> 00:16:50.876
So yeah, just 'cause I've been doing it

00:16:50.876 --> 00:16:52.015
for such a long time.

00:16:52.015 --> 00:16:54.508
The first people that I
reached out to on MySpace

00:16:54.508 --> 00:16:56.150
for the electronic producers

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:57.845
Barrio and Frank Music,

00:16:57.845 --> 00:17:00.345
and a bunch of people; Roscoe.

00:17:03.097 --> 00:17:05.239
So I've been; I think
that people appreciate

00:17:05.239 --> 00:17:07.674
that I have that connection
with electronic music

00:17:07.674 --> 00:17:12.549
and just my love of so many
different types of music.

00:17:12.549 --> 00:17:14.716
'Cause it led me to that.

